  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nA mature upper ridge remains centered over the central CONUS this\nafternoon, while a closed upper low lingers off the California\ncoast and slowly inches eastward. At the surface, weak high\npressure has shifted east with a lee trough deepening over the\nHigh Plains, supporting a southerly low-level flow regime across\nthe area. The resulting airmass is warm and dry for late\nOctober, and mixing to near the top of the boundary layer is\nallowing temperatures to climb into the upper 70s to lower 80s in\nmost locations under mostly sunny skies.\n\nSaturday and Sunday remain dominated by the ridge and a persistent\nsoutherly breeze, yielding continued warm and mainly dry weather.\nHighs both days are expected to reach around 80 degrees in much of\nthe area, with only modest day-to-day variation tied to cloud\ncover. Clouds should begin to increase from west to east on Sunday\nas the upstream trough and attendant surface front approach from\nthe northern High Plains, but forcing and deeper moisture appear\ninsufficient for meaningful precipitation during the\ndaytime Sunday. Any showers that manage to develop ahead of the\nfront would likely remain isolated and confined to far\nnorthwestern portions of the area.\n\nConfidence remains low regarding the timing and progression\nof the cold front and associated rain chances Sunday into Monday, and\nthis period continues to represent the main forecast challenge. \nEnsemble and deterministic guidance still favor a relatively slow \neastward movement of the upper trough, with the surface front \nstalling near or just northwest of the area through much of Sunday \nbefore finally pushing southeastward Sunday night into Monday.\n\nRain chances should first increase across north-central Kansas \n(Republic, Cloud, Clay, Riley, Dickinson, Geary) Sunday evening as \ndeeper ascent and moisture arrive along the boundary. Coverage there \nis most likely to reach scattered to numerous showers with embedded \nlight thunderstorms, while areas farther east and south see lower \nprobabilities and more hit-or-miss activity. Given modest instability\nand limited moisture depth, current evidence supports mainly light \nto moderate rainfall amounts, with heavier totals and any stronger \nstorms displaced to areas farther southwest where better overlap of \nforcing and moisture exists.\n\nBy Monday, the front is expected to sweep southeastward across the \nremainder of the area, shifting shower chances into east-central and \nnortheast Kansas before tapering from northwest to southeast. \nTemperatures behind the boundary should fall back into the 60s behind\nthe front and low 70s for daytime highs ahead of the front, with \nmore extensive cloud cover and any lingering showers further limiting\ndiurnal warming. Exact frontal timing remains uncertain, so the \ngradient between warm pre-frontal and cooler post-frontal \ntemperatures will still shift in subsequent forecasts.",
